<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ivory Coast</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Côte d'Ivoire</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">Ivory</a> Coast, officially Côte d'Ivoire, is a country on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-guinea">Gulf of Guinea</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>. It became a French colony in 1893 and part of French West Africa, growing into the federation's leading producer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> and cocoa, with labour drawn partly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-volta">Upper Volta</a>. When Upper Volta was abolished in 1932, much of its territory was attached to Ivory Coast until 1947. The country became independent in 1960 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/felix-houphouet-boigny">Félix Houphouët-Boigny</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ivory-coast.jpg" alt="Ivory Coast" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: See File history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_C%C3%B4te_d%27Ivoire.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Portuguese merchant-explorers of the 15th and 16th centuries divided the west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> into four coasts named for the trade each offered. The Costa do Marfim, translated into French as Côte d'Ivoire, took its name from the export of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a> and the Slave Coast did from theirs. Other names for the stretch included the Côte de Dents, the Coast of Teeth, and the Windward Coast. In the 19th century usage settled on Côte d'Ivoire, a name kept through French rule and independence in 1960.</p><p>The first human presence is hard to determine, since remains are poorly preserved in the humid climate, though polished axes and tool fragments suggest a large presence in the Upper Paleolithic period or at least the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a>. Historians believe the earliest inhabitants were displaced or absorbed by the ancestors of the present peoples, who migrated south into the area before the 16th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first recorded history comes from the chronicles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> traders, who ran a caravan trade across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> in salt, slaves and gold. Around the southern terminals of those routes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djenne">Djenné</a>, Gao and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a>, the great Sudanic empires developed, and they also became centres of Islamic education. From the 11th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> spread south into the north of what is now Ivory Coast. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana-empire">Ghana Empire</a> flourished between the 4th and 13th centuries, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a> that followed held only the northwest corner of Ivory Coast, around Odienné.</p><p>The country was traditionally home to several states, including Gyaaman, the Kong Empire and Baoulé. It became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1843 and was consolidated as a French colony in 1893 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scramble-for-africa">Scramble for Africa</a>. It achieved independence in 1960 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/felix-houphouet-boigny">Félix Houphouët-Boigny</a>, who ruled until 1993. Through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> and cocoa production it became an economic powerhouse in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> in the 1960s and 1970s. The capital has been Yamoussoukro since 1983.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Ivory Coast is a republic with strong executive power vested in its president, and its parliament consists of a Senate and a National Assembly. Since 2011 it has been organised into 12 districts and two autonomous cities. French is the official language and about 78 languages are spoken, among them Baoulé, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wangara">Dyula</a> and Bété. The citizen population falls into macroethnic groupings, chiefly the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akan">Akan</a>, Voltaic or Gur peoples, Northern Mandés, Kru-speaking peoples and Southern Mandés. Each ethnic group has its own music, mostly with strong vocal polyphony, and talking drums and polyrhythms are common.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ivory Coast” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Separate colonies were set up on the coast in Guinea, <b>Ivory Coast</b> and Dahomey.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-west-africa">French West Africa · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…through his daughter Yennenga the Mossi kingdoms of Ouagadougou, Tenkodogo, Yatenga and Fada N'Grumah, whose people are nearly half of Burkina Faso and who honour her as their mother; the Bouna kingdom of <b>Ivory Coast</b> and the Dagaaba states of Ghana's Upper West also came out of Dagbon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-dagbon">Kingdom of Dagbon · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…newly independent states in Africa and Asia adopted single-party rule, arguing that national unity and development came before competition: Ghana under Kwame Nkrumah from 1964, Tanzania under Julius Nyerere from 1965, Zambia from 1972 and Kenya from 1982, as well as Egypt, Algeria, Tunisia, <b>Ivory Coast</b> and Burma.</blockquote><a href="/government/one-party-state">One-party state · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1887-1889 the explorer Louis-Gustave Binger secured treaties in the interior, and in 1893 the <b>Ivory Coast</b> became a colony with Binger as governor.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-ivory-coast">French Ivory Coast</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1932 it was abolished and divided among the <b>Ivory Coast</b>, French Sudan and the Colony of Niger, partly to supply labour.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-upper-volta">French Upper Volta</a></li></ul></section>
